# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_ACCOUNT_CREATE: warning | critical << 32 */
int64_t account_create(uint32_t resource, uint64_t limit,
                       uint64_t warning_critical, uint32_t *out);
/* CROI_SYS_ACCOUNT_SET_LIMIT */
int64_t account_set_limit(uint32_t account, uint64_t limit);
/* CROI_SYS_PROCESS_SET_ACCOUNT */
int64_t process_set_account(uint32_t process, uint32_t account);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A memory account is one budget, in bytes, for the memory a process commits, CPU or GPU alike.
Anonymous VMOs charge their pages as they are committed; contiguous and device-local VMOs charge their whole size at once and are never evicted.
An account at its limit gives up the clean pages of its own pager VMOs, oldest first, before a commit against it fails.
A commit past the limit fails: a call gets `CROI_ERR_NO_MEMORY`, and a page fault is a fatal page fault, the process's exception.
Kernel memory (handles, channels, stacks) and page tables are not charged.

A new process charges its creator's account, unless `process_set_account` replaces it.
What the process creates charges it: vmo_create(2), the snapshots of vmo_create_child(2), vmo_create_contiguous(2) and pager_create_vmo(2), whose pages are charged as they are supplied.
`CROI_VMO_CHARGE_TO` charges another account instead, a handle with CHARGE (a client's, sent to the service that allocates for it).
A VMO keeps the account it was charged to wherever its handles go.
A process with no account is not accounted.

An account has up to two pressure levels, a warning and a critical one, as percentages of the limit.
A level is entered at its threshold and left below 80% of it.
object_bind(2) `CROI_BIND_PRESSURE` queues a `CROI_PKT_TYPE_MEMORY_PRESSURE` packet at every change of level (port(2)).
object_get_info(2) `CROI_INFO_MEMORY_ACCOUNT` gives the limit, the bytes charged, the thresholds, the level (`CROI_MEMORY_LEVEL_NORMAL`, `_WARNING`, `_CRITICAL`) and how many times it entered pressure.

The system has a root account, whose handle a process is given at startup.
Its level is the memory watchdog's, from the free memory against the watermarks the kernel was booted with, and it has two more levels, `CROI_MEMORY_LEVEL_IMMINENT_OUT_OF_MEMORY` and `CROI_MEMORY_LEVEL_OUT_OF_MEMORY`, after which the machine reboots.
Its limit is the RAM and its charge the RAM in use.
Its handle has WRITE, for binding, but not CHARGE.

## account_create

`account_create` writes to <out> a handle to a new account of <limit> bytes.
<resource> is a resource(2) that grants the MEMORY system resource.
The low 32 bits of the third argument are the warning level in percent and the high 32 the critical level; 0 turns a level off, and more than 100 is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
The handle has the basic rights, WRITE and CHARGE.

## account_set_limit

`account_set_limit` sets the limit.
The handle needs WRITE.
A limit below what is charged is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`.
The root account's is `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.

## process_set_account

`process_set_account` makes <account>, which needs CHARGE, the account of <process>, which needs WRITE.
It must be done before the process starts (`CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` after).

